# SDOC Slide Authoring Guide @slide-authoring
{
    # Meta @meta
    {
        type: skill

        sdoc-version: 0.2
    }

    # About @about
    {
        How to create presentation slides from SDOC files, and how to get
        them out as HTML, PDF or a Google Slides deck. Covers the deck
        structure, the slide properties, the plain layouts (default, center,
        two-column) and the structured ones (title, section, columns, stats,
        pipeline, matrix, rows, bars, split, stack), speaker notes, the theme
        system, and CLI usage. Read the Quick Reference for everyday
        authoring, [Structured Layouts](#structured-layouts) when a slide
        needs more than prose, and the Full Example for a complete deck
        template.
    }

    # Quick Reference @quick-reference
    {
        A slide deck is an SDOC file where each top-level scope becomes one
        slide. Set \`type: slides\` in the \`@meta\` section.

        # Minimal Deck @minimal
        {
            ```
            # My Presentation {
                @meta {
                    type: slides
                }

                # Title Slide {
                    config: center

                    Welcome to the presentation.
                }

                # Content Slide {
                    Key points:

                    {[.]
                        - First point
                        - Second point
                        - Third point
                    }
                }
            }
            ```
        }

        # Building Slides @building
        {
            Use the CLI tool:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc
            ```

            This produces \`deck.html\` — a self-contained HTML file. Open it
            in any browser. Navigate with arrow keys or swipe on touch devices.

            To specify output path:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc -o output.html
            ```

            To export as PDF:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --pdf
            ```

            This produces \`deck.pdf\` using headless Chrome, one page per
            slide, at the theme's page size. Requires Chrome or Chromium
            installed on the system.

            To export for Google Slides or PowerPoint:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --pptx
            ```

            This produces \`deck.pptx\`. See [PowerPoint and Google Slides
            Export](#pptx-export) for what it contains and how to import it.

            To choose what happens when the window is not the slide's shape:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --fit contain
            ```

            See [Aspect Ratio and Fit](#fit) for the three modes.

            To check that nothing has overflowed its slide:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --check
            ```

            This builds the HTML, measures every slide in a browser, and
            reports any whose content has run past the margin the theme
            reserved. \`--pptx\` runs the same check for free, because it has
            already measured the deck.

            To override what a format does with the optional slides:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --pdf --with-optional
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --no-optional
            ```

            A slide marked \`optional: true\` is kept in the HTML build and
            left out of \`--pdf\` and \`--pptx\`. Either default can be
            overridden in any format: \`--with-optional\` keeps them,
            \`--no-optional\` drops them. See [Optional Slides](#optional).

            To use a custom theme:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --theme path/to/theme
            ```

            Flags can be combined:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --pdf --theme path/to/theme -o presentation.pdf
            ```

            \`--pdf\` and \`--pptx\` can run in one invocation; \`-o\` then names
            the PDF and the PPTX takes the input's name.
        }

        # Slide Properties @properties
        {
            A slide scope may open with a run of \`key: value\` lines. They set
            the layout and the furniture around the content, and they must come
            before any content — a recognised key further down is ordinary
            text. Separate each with a blank line, as in \`@meta\`.

            {[table]
                Key | Effect
                \`config:\` or \`layout:\` | The layout. See [Layouts](#layouts) and [Structured Layouts](#structured-layouts).
                \`kicker:\` | The small label above the title. On a \`title\` slide it moves below the statement.
                \`lede:\` | A standfirst line under the title.
                \`footnote:\` | Small print pinned under the body: provenance, caveats, sources.
                \`status:\` | A second line under the kicker on a title slide, for a distribution notice.
                \`accent:\` | \`primary\`, \`secondary\`, \`tertiary\` or \`neutral\`. An accent names a role; the theme chooses the colour.
                \`optional:\` | \`true\` holds the slide back from the exports, and from any build passed \`--no-optional\`. See [Optional Slides](#optional).
                \`numbered:\` | \`columns\` and \`rows\`. \`true\` numbers the children 01, 02, 03.
                \`variant:\` | \`columns\` and \`rows\`. A named treatment, such as \`panel\` or \`mono\`.
                \`weights:\` | \`split\` only. The ratio between panes, as in \`48 52\`.
                \`highlight:\` | \`matrix\` only. \`last\`, or text matching the row's first cell.
                \`arrow:\` | \`pipeline\` only. The glyph drawn between steps. Defaults to an arrow.
                \`rule:\` | \`stack\` only. \`true\` draws a hairline between blocks.
                \`value:\` | On a \`rows\` or \`bars\` child: the right-hand figure.
                \`fill:\` | On a \`bars\` child: the bar length as a percentage, overriding the automatic scale.
                \`caption:\` | On a \`columns\` child: a line set under the column.
            }

            A key is only configuration where it means something. \`config:\`,
            \`kicker:\`, \`lede:\`, \`footnote:\`, \`accent:\`, \`status:\` and
            \`optional:\` are understood by every scope; the rest are understood
            only by the layout named against them above. Anywhere else they are
            ordinary text — a slide opening "Value: the customer keeps their
            data." renders that sentence, it does not silently swallow it. A
            paragraph spanning more than one source line is never
            configuration.

            No config line means the default layout: left-aligned,
            top-to-bottom.
        }

        # Speaker Notes @notes
        {
            Add a child scope with id \`notes\` inside any slide. Notes are
            hidden in the output but present in the HTML.

            ```
            # My Slide {
                Visible content.

                # Notes @notes {
                    Only the presenter sees this.
                }
            }
            ```
        }
    }

    # Slide Structure @structure
    {
        # Document Root @root
        {
            The outermost scope is the document wrapper. Its title is used as
            the HTML page title (unless overridden by \`title:\` in \`@meta\`).
            Every child scope of the root becomes one slide.

            The \`@meta\` scope is extracted and never rendered as a slide.
        }

        # Meta Properties @meta-properties
        {
            Supported properties in \`@meta\` (separate each with a blank line):

            \`type: slides\` — identifies this file as a slide deck.

            \`title: Presentation Title\` — sets the HTML page title.

            \`company: Name\` — company name, shown in the bottom-left of
            every slide.

            \`confidential: true\` — adds a confidentiality notice across
            the bottom of every slide. Uses the \`company\` name if set.
            Use \`confidential: Custom Entity\` to override with a specific
            entity name.

            \`theme: company\` — names the intended theme (informational;
            the actual theme is selected via the \`--theme\` CLI flag).

            \`author: Name\` — author name (metadata only).

            \`date: 2026-02-20\` — date (metadata only).
        }

        # What Maps to What @mapping
        {
            {[table]
                SDOC Construct | HTML Output
                Top-level scope | Slide (\`<div class="slide">\`)
                Scope heading | Slide title (\`<h2>\`)
                Paragraph | \`<p>\`
                \`{[.]}\` bullet list | \`<ul>\`
                \`{[#]}\` numbered list | \`<ol>\`
                \`{[table]}\` | \`<table>\`
                Code fence | \`<pre><code>\`
                Mermaid code fence | \`<pre class="mermaid">\` (rendered as SVG)
                SVG code fence | \`<div class="sdoc-svg-block">\` (inline SVG)
                \`**bold**\` | \`<strong>\`
                \`*italic*\` | \`<em>\`
                \`\\\`code\\\`\` | \`<code>\`
                \`[text](url)\` | \`<a>\`
                \`![alt](src)\` / \`![alt](src =50% center)\` | \`<img>\` (with optional width/alignment)
                Nested scope | \`<section>\` within the slide
                Child scope, structured layout | One cell: a column, a stat, a pipeline row, a bar, a pane
                \`kicker:\` | \`<div class="kicker">\` above the title
                \`lede:\` | \`<p class="lede">\` under the title
                \`footnote:\` | \`<div class="footnote">\` under the body
                \`accent: secondary\` | \`accent-secondary\` class on the slide or cell
                \`@notes\` child | Hidden \`<aside class="notes">\`, exported as PowerPoint speaker notes
            }
        }
    }

    # Layouts @layouts
    {
        # Default Layout @default-layout
        {
            No \`config:\` line needed. Content flows top-to-bottom,
            left-aligned. Good for most content slides.

            ```
            # Regular Slide {
                A paragraph of text.

                {[.]
                    - A bullet list
                    - With items
                }
            }
            ```
        }

        # Center Layout @center-layout
        {
            Use \`config: center\` for title slides, section dividers,
            and closing slides.

            ```
            # Welcome {
                config: center

                My Presentation Title

                A subtitle or tagline.
            }
            ```
        }

        # Beyond These Three @more-layouts
        {
            \`title\`, \`section\`, \`columns\`, \`stats\`, \`pipeline\`, \`matrix\`,
            \`rows\`, \`bars\`, \`split\` and \`stack\` consume the slide's child
            scopes and arrange them. See
            [Structured Layouts](#structured-layouts).
        }

        # Two-Column Layout @two-column-layout
        {
            Use \`config: two-column\`. Child scopes become columns.
            Good for comparisons, before/after, side-by-side content.

            ```
            # Comparison {
                config: two-column

                # Before {
                    Manual HTML slides.

                    Tedious and inconsistent.
                }

                # After {
                    Write SDOC, get slides.

                    Fast and branded.
                }
            }
            ```

            Any content before the child scopes appears above the columns.
        }
    }

    # Structured Layouts @structured-layouts
    {
        The plain layouts arrange whatever content a slide holds. A structured
        layout instead consumes the slide's *child scopes* and arranges them
        into a known shape. Each child scope becomes one cell: its heading is
        the cell's label and its content is the cell's body, with the exact
        reading depending on the layout.

        All of them are optional. A deck that uses none of them behaves exactly
        as it did before they existed.

        {[table]
            Layout | Children are | Use for
            \`title\` | — | The opening slide. The heading sets as display type and the kicker moves below it.
            \`section\` | — | A divider carrying one statement.
            \`columns\` | Columns, side by side | Parallel points, a set of figures, a row of cards, a people grid.
            \`stats\` | A figure and its caption | Two to four numbers that carry the slide.
            \`pipeline\` | Rows of chained steps | A signal path, a process, a comparison of two paths.
            \`matrix\` | — (takes a table) | A competitive comparison, with per-column accents and one row picked out.
            \`rows\` | Label, body, value rows | A layered model, a timeline, a ledger.
            \`bars\` | Labelled quantities | An allocation of money, effort or time.
            \`split\` | Two panes | Prose beside a figure, an argument beside its schedule.
            \`stack\` | Blocks, one above another | A slide carrying two layouts, such as a diagram above columns.
        }

        # Composition @layout-composition
        {
            \`split\` and \`stack\` render each child scope as a *block*, and a
            block reads its own \`config:\` line. That is how one slide carries a
            pipeline above a set of numbered columns, or prose beside a
            timeline, without a bespoke layout for the combination.

            ```
            # How a request is served {
                config: stack

                rule: true

                kicker: THE PATH

                # @paths {
                    config: pipeline

                    # Cached {
                        accent: primary

                        {[.]
                            - Request
                            - **Edge hit**
                            - Response
                        }
                    }
                }

                # @detail {
                    config: columns

                    numbered: true

                    # The hot path {
                        One hop, no origin round trip.
                    }

                    # The cold path {
                        Two hops, and the origin does the work.
                    }
                }
            }
            ```

            Nesting stops there. A block inside a block is ordinary content.
        }

        # Pipeline, and marking meaning @layout-pipeline
        {
            A pipeline row is a child scope: its heading is the row label and
            its bullet list is the steps. **A step written entirely in bold is
            marked** and takes the row's accent; every other step is neutral.

            The distinction is semantic. Where a slide sets two paths against
            each other, the marked steps are the ones a path cannot avoid and
            the unmarked ones are the stages both paths share — so the reader
            counts marks rather than reading a caption. The author marks what
            is required; the theme decides what required looks like.
            Restyling the deck must not lose that, which is why markedness
            lives in the source rather than in a colour.

            Set the row's accent with \`accent:\`. A theme may supply a default
            — the built-in theme alternates between its first two accents —
            but an explicit \`accent:\` always wins, so a deck that states its
            accents is safe against a theme change.

            \`arrow:\` sets the glyph drawn between steps, if the default arrow
            is wrong for the diagram.
        }

        # Matrix @layout-matrix
        {
            A matrix slide holds one table. Column headings alternate between
            two accents so a wide table stays scannable, and one row can be
            picked out with \`highlight:\` — either \`last\`, or text the row's
            first cell contains. A name that several rows share highlights all
            of them, so pick something specific to the row you mean.

            Within a cell, \`**bold**\` marks the name and \`*italic*\` marks the
            trailing qualifier, which themes set smaller and dimmer. That keeps
            a first column like **Beta Compute** *Series C, shipping* legible
            as two pieces of information rather than one run-on line.
        }

        # Rows and bars @layout-rows
        {
            A \`rows\` child carries up to four parts: the automatic index when
            the layout sets \`numbered: true\`, the heading as the label, the
            content as the body, and \`value:\` as the right-hand figure.
            \`variant: mono\` turns the label into a monospace accent, which is
            what makes a timeline; \`variant: ledger\` drops the body and leaves
            a label and a figure.

            A \`bars\` child is a label, a \`value:\` and a note. Bars are scaled
            against the largest numeric value in the set, so \`4,200 docs\` and
            \`270 docs\` produce the right proportion without the author
            computing percentages. Set \`fill:\` on a bar to override that.
        }

        # Images @layout-images
        {
            Images use the ordinary inline form, \`![alt](path)\`, inside
            whichever cell they belong to. In a \`columns\` slide with
            \`variant: card\`, an image at the top of each column with a heading
            and a line of body beneath gives the standard figure row.

            Paths are resolved relative to the \`.sdoc\` file. They are embedded
            in the PPTX export; a remote URL is left out of it, and the build
            says which.
        }
    }

    # Drilldown Slides @drilldown
    {
        A slide deck is normally a 1D spine: Right and Left move along it. A
        spine slide can also have *vertical detail slides* — extra material
        you can pull up on demand without cluttering the main flow. Mark a
        child scope with the `:detail` annotation and it becomes a vertical
        slide under its parent.

        # Syntax @drilldown-syntax
        {
            ```
            # How the cache works @cache {
                Main spine content.

                # Eviction policy @eviction :detail {
                    Detail slide one.
                }

                # Cold-start behaviour @cold-start :detail {
                    Detail slide two.
                }

                # Notes @notes {
                    Speaker notes still work alongside details.
                }
            }
            ```

            A detail slide is a regular slide. It can have its own `config:
            center`, two-column layout, speaker `@notes`, and any other slide
            content. Drilldowns do not nest further: a `:detail` inside a
            `:detail` is treated as a plain nested scope.
        }

        # Navigation @drilldown-navigation
        {
            Right — advance one step. From a spine slide, move to the next
            spine. From a detail slide, advance to the next detail in the
            same column; at the last detail, return to the parent spine and
            advance to the next spine (reveal.js convention).

            Left — mirror of Right. From a detail, previous detail or back
            to parent spine; from spine, previous spine.

            Down — drill into the first detail of the current spine; from
            within a column, advance to the next detail.

            Up — return from a detail to its parent spine. No-op on a spine.

            Swipe — horizontal swipes mirror Left / Right; vertical swipes
            mirror Up / Down.

            URL hash — spine slides use `#N`; detail K of spine N uses
            `#N.K`.
        }

        # Visual Affordances @drilldown-visual
        {
            Spine slides that have detail children get the
            `slide-has-details` class — the default theme shows a small
            animated chevron at the bottom of the slide. Themes can replace
            this rule to fit their own visual language.

            Every slide also gets a `.slide-indicator` element in the
            bottom-right corner: `5 / 14` on a spine, `5.2 / 14` on detail 2
            of slide 5. The denominator is always the spine count, so the
            indicator stays stable as you drill in.
        }

        # PDF Export @drilldown-pdf
        {
            PDF export flattens the deck: spine 1, its details in order,
            spine 2, its details in order, and so on. This is the same order
            slides appear in the HTML, so PDF export needs no special case —
            every slide is just one page.

            Every detail is a page, then, including the ones the audience
            never saw. Mark a detail `optional: true` to keep it out of the
            exported file — see [Optional Slides](#optional).
        }
    }

    # Optional Slides @optional
    {
        Some slides exist for the room and not for the file that gets sent
        on: the answer to a question that may not be asked, the figure
        behind a figure, the rebuttal you hope to leave unused. Set
        \`optional: true\` on the slide and it stays in the HTML build, which
        is what you present from, and leaves the PDF and the PPTX, which are
        what you send.

        # Syntax @optional-syntax
        {
            ```
            # Unit economics @unit-economics {
                Headline numbers.

                # Gross margin by cohort @margin :detail {
                    optional: true

                    The cohort table, if anyone asks for it.
                }
            }

            # Pricing objections @objections {
                optional: true

                Held back unless pricing comes up.
            }
            ```

            \`optional:\` is a slide property, not a scope annotation, and that
            is deliberate: a heading carries one \`:type\`, and a drilldown has
            already spent it on \`:detail\`. Reading optional-ness off the
            configuration run keeps the two independent, so all four
            combinations are available — a required spine, an optional spine,
            a required detail, an optional detail.

            A slide is the unit. Setting it on a cell inside a structured
            layout — a column, a pipeline row, a pane of a \`split\` — has no
            effect: the whole slide travels or none of it does. Mark the slide
            that holds the cell.

            It is set the way the other boolean properties are: \`true\`,
            \`yes\`, \`on\` or \`1\` mark the slide; \`false\`, \`no\`, \`off\` or \`0\`
            leave it alone. Like every slide property it must come before any
            content.

            A value that is none of those words is not configuration at all.
            A slide whose first line is "Optional: a second seat costs
            nothing" renders that sentence — a boolean key discards what it
            cannot read, so it declines the line rather than swallowing it.
        }

        # What each format does @optional-formats
        {
            {[table]
                Format | Default | Why
                HTML | Included | It is the format you present from, and an optional slide is one you may want in the room.
                PDF | Excluded | It is a format you send on.
                PPTX | Excluded | The same.
            }

            The default is a guess about intent, not a property of the format,
            so either can be overridden anywhere. An HTML deck is also
            something you send someone:

            ```
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --pdf --with-optional
            node tools/build-slides.js deck.sdoc --no-optional
            ```

            When both are given the last one wins, as with \`--fit\`.

            An optional spine takes its details with it. A detail belongs to
            its spine, and there is nowhere for it to stand once the spine is
            gone, so it goes whether or not it is marked itself.
        }

        # Numbering @optional-numbering
        {
            Optional slides are dropped before the deck is numbered, not
            hidden afterwards, so the slide indicator counts what the reader
            actually holds. A deck of four spines with one of them optional
            reads \`3 / 3\` on its last page in the exported file and \`4 / 4\`
            in the HTML build. The pages are consecutive either way; there is
            no gap where a slide was removed.

            A spine whose only details are optional loses its
            \`slide-has-details\` class in the export too, so no theme draws a
            drilldown chevron pointing at nothing.
        }

        # Themes @optional-theme
        {
            An optional slide carries the \`slide-optional\` class wherever it
            is kept. A theme can use it to mark the slide on screen — a corner
            tab, a tinted rule — so the presenter can see which slides are off
            the main path. The built-in theme does not style it, so an optional
            slide looks like any other until a theme says otherwise.

            Nothing marked optional reaches a build it was excluded from, so
            the class cannot appear in one.
        }
    }

    # Theme System @themes
    {
        Themes control the visual appearance of slides. A theme is a directory
        containing:

        \`theme.css\` — all visual styling (typography, colours, spacing, layouts).

        \`theme.js\` (optional) — runtime behaviour (keyboard nav, touch support,
        slide counter). A theme that ships none inherits the default runtime,
        so most themes should omit it.

        \`theme.json\` (optional) — the design box and the print page.

        Assets (optional) — fonts and images the CSS refers to, in any
        subdirectory.

        # The design box @theme-box
        {
            Slides are laid out at a fixed size and the whole slide is then
            scaled to fill the window, so the author's layout is preserved
            verbatim at every window size and the screen and the PDF are the
            same geometry by construction.

            \`theme.json\` declares that size, and may declare the default fit
            mode (see [Aspect Ratio and Fit](#fit)). The box in CSS pixels and
            the page in inches must agree at 96 dpi, or the PDF clips:

            ```
            {
              "name": "wide-dark",
              "slide": { "width": 1920, "height": 1080 },
              "page":  { "width": 20, "height": 11.25 },
              "fit":   "contain",
              "fonts": { "display": "Georgia", "body": "Helvetica" }
            }
            ```

            With no \`theme.json\` a theme gets 1280 by 720, which is 13.333 by
            7.5 inches — the same 16:9 proportion.

            \`fonts\` names the typefaces the PPTX export falls back to when a
            CSS font stack resolves to a generic family. It has no effect on
            the HTML.

            Every length in a theme's CSS is a pixel value at the design size.
            A theme written against a 1920 by 1080 box sets a 68px heading and
            means 68px on a 1920-wide slide, whatever the window is doing.
        }

        # Fonts and other assets @theme-assets
        {
            A theme may ship web fonts, background images or textures beside
            its CSS and refer to them with ordinary relative URLs:

            ```
            @font-face {
              font-family: "Deck Sans";
              src: url("fonts/deck-sans-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
            }
            ```

            The builder rewrites every relative \`url()\` in \`theme.css\` to a
            \`data:\` URI, so the built deck is one file that carries its own
            typefaces. Ship only fonts whose licence allows redistribution —
            the SIL Open Font License does. It renders the same offline, on a machine without the
            fonts installed, and inside headless Chrome during export —
            which matters, because a PDF built against a fallback font is a
            PDF with the wrong line breaks.

            Absolute URLs, protocol-relative URLs and existing \`data:\` URIs
            are left alone, and a path escaping the theme directory is
            refused and reported.
        }

        # Writing against the layouts @theme-classes
        {
            A slide carries \`layout-<name>\` for whatever \`config:\` named, plus
            \`accent-<name>\` if the slide set one. Target those. The bare layout
            name is also emitted for \`center\` and \`two-column\` alone, because
            themes written before \`layout-*\` existed rely on it; a theme that
            styled some other layout by its bare name must move to
            \`layout-<name>\`.

            The head and body are wrapped in \`.slide-head\` and \`.slide-body\`,
            both \`display: contents\` by default, so the *boxes* a theme sees
            are the boxes it always saw: the heading and the content lay out
            as direct children of the slide. A theme that wants a real header
            band, or a body that takes the remaining height, overrides those
            two rules.

            **The DOM did change, even though the layout did not.**
            \`display: contents\` removes a box, not an element, so a selector
            written with a child combinator no longer matches: \`.slide > h2\`
            and \`.slide > p\` have to become \`.slide h2\` and \`.slide p\`, or
            name the wrapper. The same goes for \`:first-child\` and
            \`:nth-child\` counted over a slide's children. Descendant
            selectors are unaffected.

            Structured layouts emit stable class names inside the body:
            \`.columns > .column\`, \`.stats > .stat\`, \`.pipeline > .pipe-row\`,
            \`.matrix\`, \`.rows > .row\`, \`.bars > .bar\`, \`.split > .pane\`,
            \`.stack > .stack-block\`. A theme is free to style only the ones it
            uses.
        }

        # Built-in Default Theme @default-theme
        {
            Used when no \`--theme\` flag is provided. Clean, minimal styling
            with keyboard navigation and touch swipe support.
        }

        # Custom Themes @custom-themes
        {
            Create a directory with \`theme.css\` and optionally \`theme.js\`,
            then pass it via the CLI:

            ```
            --theme path/to/your/theme
            ```
        }

        # Navigation Controls @navigation
        {
            All themes include these controls by default:

            Arrow right or Space — next slide (or next detail).

            Arrow left — previous slide (or previous detail).

            Arrow down — drill into details (if any).

            Arrow up — return from details to the spine slide.

            Home — first slide.

            End — last spine slide.

            Touch swipe horizontal / vertical on mobile devices.

            Slide counter (`.slide-indicator`) shown in the bottom-right
            corner — `N / TOTAL` on spine slides, `N.K / TOTAL` on detail K
            of spine N. See [Drilldown Slides](#drilldown) for details.
        }
    }

    # Aspect Ratio and Fit @fit
    {
        A deck is laid out at one fixed size — the theme's design box — and the
        whole slide is then scaled to meet the window. A window is rarely the
        slide's exact shape, and what happens in that case is a build-time
        choice:

        {[table]
            \`--fit\` | What happens off-ratio | Cost
            \`contain\` | Scale to fit; the remainder is letterboxed | Unused screen at the edges
            \`cover\` | Scale to fill; the overflow is cropped | Content near an edge is lost, including the footer
            \`stretch\` | Scale each axis to the window | The slide is distorted
        }

        \`contain\` is the default and is almost always the right answer: the
        deck keeps the proportions it was designed in, and nothing is lost or
        misshapen. A theme may set its own default with \`"fit"\` in
        \`theme.json\`, and \`--fit\` on the command line overrides it.

        # Make the letterbox visible @fit-letterbox
        {
            With \`contain\`, the area the slide does not cover is painted with
            \`--sdoc-letterbox\`. **A theme must not leave that equal to its own
            slide background.** If it does, the slide has no visible edge, and
            on an off-ratio window anything pinned to the bottom of the slide —
            the footer, a footnote — appears to float in the middle of the
            window instead of sitting on the slide's edge. Nothing is
            misplaced; there is simply no edge to see it against.

            Set the two apart:

            ```
            :root { --sdoc-letterbox: #020202; }   /* behind the slide */
            body   { background: var(--sdoc-letterbox); }
            .slide { background: #101010; }        /* the slide itself */
            ```

            A hairline helps further on a dark theme, where the two grounds are
            necessarily close:

            ```
            .slide { box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px #1E1E1E; }
            ```
        }

        # For theme runtimes @fit-runtime
        {
            The renderer writes the mode to \`<html data-sdoc-fit>\` and the
            theme runtime reads it, publishing \`--sdoc-slide-scale\` and
            \`--sdoc-slide-scale-y\` on \`:root\`. Only \`stretch\` ever sets them
            to different values.

            \`--sdoc-slide-scale-y\` defaults to \`--sdoc-slide-scale\`, so a
            theme shipping its own \`theme.js\` that writes only the one
            variable still scales uniformly rather than collapsing vertically.
        }
    }

    # PowerPoint and Google Slides Export @pptx-export
    {
        \`--pptx\` writes a PowerPoint file. Drive imports it as a native Google
        Slides deck: upload it, then **File > Open with > Google Slides**, or
        drag it into \`slides.google.com\`. That is the export route rather than
        the Slides API because it needs no credentials in the toolchain, and
        because the result is a useful file on its own.

        # How it works @pptx-mechanism
        {
            The exporter has no layout engine. It builds the HTML deck, opens
            it in headless Chrome, and asks the browser where every box, rule,
            image and run of text ended up. Each of those becomes one
            absolutely positioned shape in the exported file.

            The consequence worth knowing: **the CSS is the single source of
            layout truth**. A new layout, a new theme, a change to a margin —
            all of it exports correctly without the exporter being taught
            anything, because the exporter only ever reads the result.

            Absolute positioning is also how a designed deck is built by hand.
            The exported file is loose shapes on one blank layout, which is
            what a designer would hand over, and it stays editable in Slides.
        }

        # What carries across @pptx-fidelity
        {
            Position, size, typeface, size, weight, italic, colour, letter
            spacing, alignment and exact line spacing, per run. Fills,
            borders, corner radii and alpha. Images, embedded from local paths
            or \`data:\` URIs. Slide backgrounds. Speaker notes, as notes.

            A border on only some sides is exported as a filled bar per side
            rather than an outlined box, so a hairline under a heading is a
            hairline rather than a rectangle with three invisible edges.

            What is not in the file at all: slides marked \`optional: true\`,
            unless \`--with-optional\` was passed. See
            [Optional Slides](#optional).

            What does not carry: web fonts are named, not embedded, so the
            fonts a theme uses must be available to the viewer. Choosing faces
            that are on Google Fonts is the reliable route, because Slides
            resolves those. Remote images are skipped and reported. Mermaid
            diagrams and KaTeX export as the text and shapes the browser drew,
            not as editable objects.
        }
    }

    # Full Example @full-example
    {
        A complete slide deck demonstrating all features:

        ````
        # Product Update — Q1 2026 {
            @meta {
                type: slides

                title: Product Update Q1 2026

                author: Team Lead

                date: 2026-03-15
            }

            # Product Update {
                config: center

                Q1 2026
            }

            # What We Shipped {
                Key deliverables this quarter:

                {[.]
                    - New authentication system
                    - Dashboard redesign
                    - API v2 launch
                }

                # Notes @notes {
                    Mention the 40% performance improvement.
                }
            }

            # Metrics {
                {[table]
                    Metric | Q4 2025 | Q1 2026
                    Users | 12,000 | 18,500
                    API calls/day | 1.2M | 2.1M
                    Uptime | 99.9% | 99.95%
                }
            }

            # Before and After {
                config: two-column

                # Before {
                    Manual deployments.

                    2-hour release cycles.

                    Frequent rollbacks.
                }

                # After {
                    Automated CI/CD.

                    15-minute releases.

                    Zero rollbacks this quarter.
                }
            }

            # Architecture {
                The new system:

                ```mermaid
                graph LR
                  Client --> Gateway[API Gateway]
                  Gateway --> Auth[Auth Service]
                  Gateway --> Core[Core Service]
                  Gateway --> Analytics
                ```
            }

            # What's Next {
                Q2 priorities:

                {[#]
                    - Mobile app launch
                    - Internationalisation
                    - Enterprise SSO
                }
            }

            # Thank You {
                config: center

                Questions?
            }
        }
        ````
    }

    # Common Mistakes @common-mistakes
    {
        **Forgetting blank lines between meta properties.** SDOC joins
        consecutive lines into a single paragraph. Put a blank line between
        each \`key: value\` pair in \`@meta\`:

        ```
        # Wrong — these become one paragraph:
        @meta {
            type: slides
            title: My Deck
        }

        # Correct:
        @meta {
            type: slides

            title: My Deck
        }
        ```

        **Putting config after content.** The \`config:\` line must appear
        before any content in the slide scope. If it appears after a paragraph,
        it will be rendered as regular text.

        **Using config on the document root.** \`config:\` only works on slide
        scopes (direct children of the root), and on child scopes in a
        \`split\` or \`stack\` — not on the root itself.

        **Putting content in a structured layout's slide scope.** In
        \`columns\`, \`stats\`, \`rows\` and the rest, the *child scopes* are the
        content. A paragraph written directly in the slide scope appears above
        the structure, which is occasionally what you want and usually not —
        \`lede:\` is the way to put a line under the title.

        **Expecting a bullet list to become a pipeline.** A \`pipeline\` slide's
        steps are the items of a bullet list inside a *child scope*, one scope
        per row. A list written directly in the slide scope is just a list.

        **Forgetting the @notes id.** Speaker notes require the \`@notes\` id
        on the child scope heading. Without it, the scope renders as a visible
        section.
    }
}
